module gunbc.live_deploy.belt_cadence

import std.measure { Second, second }

// OnUnitInactiveSec, NOT OnUnitActiveSec, and the difference is a fail-closed choice rather than a
// formatting one. OnUnitActiveSec measures from when the tick STARTED, so a tick that runs longer
// than the cadence schedules its successor while it is still running and ticks pile up back-to-back
// — for a unit whose job is spawning agent sessions, that converts a slow tick into overlapping
// spawns. OnUnitInactiveSec measures from when the previous tick FINISHED, so the gap is real
// regardless of how long a tick takes and the belt can never outrun itself. systemd additionally
// refuses to start a Type=oneshot unit that is already active, so the two together make overlap
// unwritable rather than unlikely.
//
// 60s is the operator's stated default for this lane and is deliberately not tuned against
// anything: a tick against an unchanged frontier is an idempotent reconcile that observes live tmux
// sessions and does nothing (belt_run_once_note), so the cost of a too-fast cadence is an
// observation, not an action. AccuracySec=5s keeps systemd from coalescing the timer into a distant
// wakeup window, which is the default behavior and would otherwise make the effective cadence
// unpredictable.
//
// OnBootSec=2min rather than firing immediately: at boot the tree sync, the serve unit and
// tailscale are all converging, and a belt tick that spawns before the repo tree is in place
// produces SpawnFailed rows for a condition that resolves itself within seconds. The delay costs
// one cadence period and removes a class of self-inflicted refusals from the receipt.

data belt_tick_cadence: Second = second(count: 60)

// THE PER-TICK WATCHDOG, and the 2026-09-21 measurement that put it here. A Type=oneshot unit with
// no start bound runs as long as the tick runs, and OnUnitInactiveSec only schedules the next tick
// from INACTIVITY -- so one tick that wedged in the SCM seed (measured that night: 2.25 hours at
// 100% CPU inside one capture, the timer showing no next elapse and no receipt distinguishing
// "nothing to do" from "stuck") halted the whole pipeline invisibly; the operator could not tell
// the frozen cadence from an idle belt. TimeoutStartSec is the bounded-reconciliation backstop in
// the workflow-execution ruling's sense: past the bound systemd kills the tick, the unit goes
// inactive(failed) LOUDLY, and the timer resumes on the next cadence -- one lost pass, never a
// lost night. The bound must clear the worst HONEST tick, which is first-capture-heavy: a fresh
// project envelope seeds the whole ~7,050-path intake base through the git bridge once (measured
// 2026-09-21 at roughly seven minutes of bridge time per project), and verification then runs the
// node's validations; it must stay far below the hours a wedge needs to be noticed by a human.
// The bound names the instrument that re-derives it, never a transcribed number (DESIGN section 6):
// a green tick's receipt at receipts/belt-last-tick.json carries its observed_at, and the belt's
// own stage timings re-derive the worst honest pass. 2700s (45 minutes) is the policy row: roughly six times
// the 2026-09-21 measured seed-bridge cost, far below the hours a wedge needs to be noticed by a
// human, and any honest growth beyond it must move this row with a reason, not the wedge.
data belt_tick_run_bound: Second = second(count: 2700)
